>
  <section class="brx-sec brx-tint" id="cost">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>How much does outside tap installation cost in Liverpool?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">A standard outside tap costs £145 with us, supplied and fitted, including VAT. That is a fixed price, with no call-out fee.</p>
      <p>The price covers most homes. We take the water from your kitchen cold supply. Then we run a short pipe through the outside wall to the tap. That is up to about 2m of pipe.</p>
      <p>Some jobs need more work. We always tell you the price before we start.</p>
      <div class="brx-table-wrap">
        <table class="brx-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Job</th><th>Price, inc VAT</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><strong>Cold outside tap, short run from the kitchen</strong></td><td>£145 fixed</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Longer pipe run or a different wall</strong></td><td>Priced before we start</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Hot and cold outside taps</strong></td><td>Priced before we start</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Leaking, frozen or stiff tap repair</strong></td><td>£120 first hour, weekdays 9am–4pm</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Repair in the evening, at night or at the weekend</strong></td><td>£150 first hour (£180 bank holidays)</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>Have some other small jobs? Make a list. Many fit into the same visit, and we tell you the price before we start. See our <a href="/small-plumbing-jobs-liverpool">small plumbing jobs</a> page.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec" id="included">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>What is included when you fit an outside tap?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">Everything you need for a safe, working garden tap. You do not need to buy any parts.</p>
      <div class="sg-cols">
        <div class="sg-box">
          <h3>The parts</h3>
          <ul class="sg-ticks">
            <li>A standard garden tap with a hose fitting</li>
            <li>A double check valve, to stop backflow</li>
            <li>An isolation valve inside the house</li>
            <li>Copper pipe and fittings, up to about 2m</li>
            <li>Pipe clips and a sealed hole through the wall</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="sg-box">
          <h3>The work</h3>
          <ul class="sg-ticks">
            <li>Finding the best spot for the tap</li>
            <li>Drilling the wall and sealing it after</li>
            <li>Testing every joint for leaks</li>
            <li>Showing you how to drain it for winter</li>
            <li>Tidying up before we leave</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="brx-note"><p><strong>Why the double check valve matters.</strong> A hose left in a bucket or pond can pull dirty water back into the pipe. The water rules say a garden tap must be protected against this. Some cheap DIY kits leave it out. Ours never do.</p></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec brx-tint" id="how">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>How do you fit an outside tap?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">Most outside taps are fitted in 1 to 2 hours. Here is what happens.</p>
      <ol class="sg-steps">
        <li><strong>Call us.</strong> Tell us where you want the tap and where your kitchen sink is. We confirm the price. (5 minutes)</li>
        <li><strong>We plan the route.</strong> On the day, we find the nearest cold supply and agree the spot with you. (10 minutes)</li>
        <li><strong>We fit the valves.</strong> We turn the water off and add the isolation valve and the double check valve. (20–30 minutes)</li>
        <li><strong>We drill and run the pipe.</strong> We drill through the wall, run the pipe and fix the tap firmly. (30–45 minutes)</li>
        <li><strong>We test it.</strong> The water goes back on. We check every joint for leaks and run the tap. (10 minutes)</li>
        <li><strong>We show you.</strong> You see how to turn it off and drain it for winter. Then we tidy up.</li>
      </ol>
      <div class="brx-cta">
        <p><strong>Want an outside tap this week?</strong> Call and we will find the next free slot.</p>
        <a href={TEL} class="brx-btn">Call {PHONE}</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec" id="which">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>Which outside tap option is right for me?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">For watering the garden or washing the car, a standard cold tap at £145 is all most people need.</p>
      <div class="brx-table-wrap">
        <table class="brx-table">
          <thead><tr><th>If you want to...</th><th>Best choice</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Water plants or fill a paddling pool</td><td>Standard cold garden tap</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Use a hose or pressure washer</td><td>Standard cold garden tap</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Wash a dog, bins or muddy boots in warm water</td><td>Hot and cold outside taps</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Have a tap at the front and the back</td><td>Two taps, priced together</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Fix an old tap that drips or sticks</td><td>Repair, or swap it for a new tap</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <h3>Where should the tap go?</h3>
      <p>The cheapest spot is on the outside of the kitchen wall, near the sink. That keeps the pipe short. A tap on another wall works too, but it needs more pipe.</p>
      <p>Many Liverpool terraces have a back yard behind the kitchen. That makes a tap in the yard quick and simple. In semis in areas like Allerton, Childwall and West Derby, the kitchen is often at the back, near the garden.</p>
      <h3>Want an outside socket too?</h3>
      <p>A pressure washer or garden lights need power as well as water. Our partner firm <a href="https://www.idealelectricalsolutions.co.uk/">Ideal Electrical Solutions</a> fits outdoor sockets.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec brx-tint" id="repair">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>Is your outside tap leaking or frozen?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">We fix leaking, frozen and burst outside taps. The first hour is £120 on weekdays, with no call-out fee.</p>
      <div class="sg-cols">
        <div class="sg-box">
          <h3>Common faults</h3>
          <ul class="brx-list">
            <li><strong>Drips from the spout:</strong> usually a worn washer.</li>
            <li><strong>Leaks round the handle:</strong> often a worn gland or seal.</li>
            <li><strong>Water behind the wall:</strong> a split pipe or failed joint.</li>
            <li><strong>Tap will not turn:</strong> stiff with age or limescale.</li>
            <li><strong>No water at all:</strong> the pipe may be frozen, or the valve is off.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="sg-box">
          <h3>What we do</h3>
          <ul class="sg-ticks">
            <li>Find the cause and tell you the fix</li>
            <li>Price any parts before we fit them</li>
            <li>Replace the tap if it is not worth repairing</li>
            <li>Add a double check valve if it is missing</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="brx-danger">
        <p><strong>Burst pipe or a tap that will not stop?</strong> Turn off the isolation valve inside. If there is none, turn off your stop tap, often under the kitchen sink. Then call <a href="tel:01515580334">{PHONE}</a>. We are typically with you within 30 minutes.</p>
      </div>
      <p>Water inside the house from a split pipe? Our <a href="/leak-detection-liverpool">leak detection</a> service finds hidden leaks and can provide a report for your insurer.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec" id="winter">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>How do I stop my outside tap freezing?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">Turn off the valve inside and drain the tap before the first frost. Then fit a frost cover.</p>
      <ol class="sg-steps">
        <li><strong>Turn off the isolation valve</strong> inside the house.</li>
        <li><strong>Open the outside tap</strong> and let the water run out.</li>
        <li><strong>Unplug the hose,</strong> so water cannot sit in the tap.</li>
        <li><strong>Close the tap</strong> and fit an insulated frost cover.</li>
        <li><strong>Turn the valve back on</strong> in spring.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>Water swells as it freezes. That can split the pipe or the tap. You may not see it until the thaw, when water starts to leak behind the wall.</p>
      <p>Is it frozen already? Turn off the valve inside and open the tap. Warm the pipe gently with a cloth soaked in warm water. Never use a flame, a heat gun or boiling water.</p>
      <h3>Who does the work?</h3>
      <p>Our engineers are part of our team led by James. Every outside tap we fit comes with a 12-month workmanship guarantee. We carry £2 million public liability insurance and give you a fixed price before we start.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</ServiceGuide>
